**for vs while.** Both repeat a block while a condition is true; they are interchangeable in power.
- `for (init; condition; update)` puts initialisation, test and update in one line. It is best when the number of iterations is known or you count with an index.
- `while (condition)` only has the test; setup and update are written separately. It suits situations where the number of iterations is unknown, such as reading input until end-of-file or looping until a condition changes.
- `do-while` is a variant that runs the body at least once.
- In both, an omitted/always-true condition (`for(;;)` or `while(1)`) is an infinite loop.

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += a[i];
    while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1) sum += x;

**switch-case vs if-else.**
- `if-else` can test any boolean expression: ranges (`x > 10`), compound conditions (`a && b`), floats, strings (via comparison functions). Conditions are checked in order.
- `switch` compares **one integral expression** (int, char, enum) against constant case labels. It cannot test ranges or floating-point values and case labels must be constants.
- `switch` needs `break` to stop; without it execution **falls through** to the next case. A `default` label handles everything else.
- Readability: `switch` is cleaner for many fixed values (menu choices, days of the week). Compilers may turn it into a jump table, so lookup can be faster than a long if-else chain, but do not choose it for speed alone.
- Use `if-else` for ranges and complex logic.

    switch (choice) {
        case 1: printf("Add"); break;
        case 2: printf("Delete"); break;
        default: printf("Invalid");
    }

**Summary line to say:** choose `for` when you know the count, `while` when you loop on a condition, `switch` for discrete constant choices, and `if-else` for everything else.
